WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.292
Webhook tests should cover more than a valid happy path.

2
00:00:03.292 --> 00:00:24.094
Useful tests include accepted valid signatures, rejected invalid
signatures, missing signature headers, stale timestamps, duplicate event
identifiers, unsupported event types, malformed payloads, oversized
payloads, wrong content types, tenant-boundary failures, authorization
failures, retry scenarios, and idempotent reprocessing.

3
00:00:24.094 --> 00:00:30.327
These tests give teams confidence that the handler fails safely when
integration assumptions are wrong.

4
00:00:30.327 --> 00:00:34.740
Logging should support operations without exposing sensitive material.

5
00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:44.965
Webhook logs may include event names, provider identifiers, local
correlation identifiers, validation outcome, processing status, latency,
and safe error categories.

6
00:00:44.965 --> 00:00:56.802
They should avoid shared secrets, tokens, signatures, full sensitive
payloads, unnecessary personal data, raw authorization headers, and
anything that would make replay or impersonation easier.

7
00:00:56.802 --> 00:01:01.915
Redaction should be part of the logging design, not an afterthought after
an incident.

8
00:01:01.915 --> 00:01:05.487
Monitoring should make abnormal webhook behavior visible.

9
00:01:05.487 --> 00:01:19.565
Track failed validations, unknown event types, invalid signatures, stale
events, duplicate rates, processing delays, queue depth, retry storms,
downstream failures, unusually high event volume, and sudden drops in
expected events.

10
00:01:19.565 --> 00:01:28.320
Good monitoring helps distinguish provider outages, integration bugs,
credential problems, malicious traffic, and local processing failures.

11
00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:33.923
Without telemetry, webhook systems can silently lose events or repeat
actions for a long time.

12
00:01:33.923 --> 00:01:40.787
Incident response should include exposed secrets, unsafe processing, data
leakage, and incorrect state changes.

13
00:01:40.787 --> 00:01:53.044
Teams need a plan to rotate or revoke signing secrets, coordinate with
providers, pause or throttle processing, replay or reconcile events
safely, review logs, assess data impact, and add regression tests.

14
00:01:53.044 --> 00:01:57.246
After an incident, the durable improvement is not only a fixed handler.

15
00:01:57.246 --> 00:02:04.040
It is clearer ownership, better validation, safer logging, stronger
monitoring, and documented recovery steps.
